```js
function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  let timer = null;

  return function (...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const remaining = wait - (now - last);

    if (remaining <= 0) {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      timer = null;
      last = now;
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else if (!timer) {
      // Trailing call so the final scroll position isn't missed
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        last = Date.now();
        timer = null;
        fn.apply(this, args);
      }, remaining);
    }
  };
}

const onScroll = throttle(() => {
  console.log('scrollY:', window.scrollY);
}, 200);

window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
```

The handler fires immediately on the first scroll event, then at most once per 200 ms while scrolling continues. The trailing timer guarantees one last call after scrolling stops, so you always process the final scroll position.

`{ passive: true }` tells the browser the handler won't call `preventDefault()`, so scrolling isn't blocked waiting on it.

To remove it later, use `window.removeEventListener('scroll', onScroll)` with the same throttled reference.

If you already use Lodash, `_.throttle(fn, 200)` does the same thing.
